          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How often does guidance describe checking thyroid function once treatment is stable?",
          "answer": "Guidelines typically recommend monitoring thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) levels six to eight weeks after starting levothyroxine therapy or after any dose change, as this allows enough time to establish a new steady state (source: \"Hypothyroidism: which test comes first, and the monitoring interval after a change,\" Endocrinology and thyroid association guidance).\n\nOnce treatment is stable and there have been no dose adjustments, routine TSH checks can often be done every six to twelve months, but specific intervals may vary based on individual clinical circumstances and differing guidelines among authorities. Always discuss your monitoring schedule with your healthcare clinician to tailor it to your specific situation.\n\nIf you have further concerns, consider consulting a healthcare professional.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
